# 1 SNS filter policies - D
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["Amazon SNS", "Amazon SQS", "Message filtering"],
p="An e-commerce company publishes all order events to a single Amazon SNS standard topic. Each event contains an orderType attribute with values such as retail, wholesale, or subscription. Three Amazon SQS queues are subscribed to the topic, but each consuming service needs only one order type and currently discards the other messages after receiving them, which wastes compute and increases costs. The company wants to fix this with the least change to the publishers and consumers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
o=["Create a separate SNS topic for each order type, subscribe each queue to its topic, and update the publishers to choose the correct topic for each event.",
   "Replace the SQS queues with an Amazon Kinesis Data Streams stream and use the order type as the partition key.",
   "Configure each consuming service to use SQS long polling with a message attribute name filter when receiving messages.",
   "Add a subscription filter policy on each SQS subscription that matches only the orderType value that the service needs."],
c="D",
w=[("Filtering at the topic", "SNS subscription filter policies evaluate message attributes (or the message body) and deliver only matching messages to each subscriber."),
   ("No code changes", "Publishers keep publishing to one topic, and consumers simply stop receiving irrelevant messages."),
   ("Lower cost", "Unwanted messages are never delivered to the queues, so consumers do not spend compute discarding them.")],
x={"A": "Separate topics push routing logic into the publishers, increasing coupling and requiring publisher changes.",
   "B": "Kinesis Data Streams does not filter records per consumer; every consumer would still read all records.",
   "C": "The MessageAttributeNames parameter only selects which attributes are returned; it does not filter which messages are received."},
e=[("Pub/sub with per-subscriber subsets", "SNS fan-out to SQS with subscription filter policies (or EventBridge rules)."),
   ("Loose coupling", "Publishers should not need to know which consumers exist.")]))
# 2 WebSocket - A
