# 6 DynamoDB Streams - A
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["Amazon DynamoDB", "DynamoDB Streams", "AWS Lambda", "Event-driven architecture"],
p="A company stores customer orders in an Amazon DynamoDB table that is written to by several applications. Whenever an order item is inserted or its status changes, a loyalty service must update points and a notification must be sent. The company does not want to modify every writing application, and changes for the same order must be processed in order. Which solution meets these requirements?",
o=["Enable DynamoDB Streams on the table and process the stream with an AWS Lambda function.",
   "Schedule an AWS Lambda function every minute that scans the table for items with a recent last-modified timestamp.",
   "Modify each writing application to publish an event to an Amazon SNS topic after writing to the table.",
   "Add DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) in front of the table and subscribe the loyalty service to cache updates."],
c="A",
w=[("Change data capture", "DynamoDB Streams captures every item-level insert, update, and delete without changes to the writers."),
   ("Ordering", "Stream records for the same item (partition key) appear in order, and Lambda processes each shard in order."),
   ("Event-driven", "The Lambda event source mapping polls the stream and invokes the function automatically.")],
x={"B": "Scanning every minute is inefficient, consumes read capacity, and can miss intermediate changes.",
   "C": "Modifying every writer is exactly what the company wants to avoid, and dual writes can become inconsistent.",
   "D": "DAX is a read cache; it does not publish change events."},
e=[("React to DynamoDB item changes", "DynamoDB Streams + Lambda (or Kinesis Data Streams for DynamoDB / EventBridge Pipes)."),
   ("Avoid dual writes", "Use change data capture so that data and events stay consistent.")]))
# 7 caching TWO (C, E)
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["Amazon ElastiCache", "Caching strategies", "Lazy loading"],
p="A company's product catalog runs on Amazon RDS for MySQL. Reads outnumber writes 50 to 1, and most reads target a small fraction of popular products. Product pages can show data that is up to 5 minutes old. Traffic is spiky and unpredictable, and the company wants to cache only the data that is actually requested while minimizing the effort of capacity planning for the cache. Which combination of actions will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
o=["Use a write-through strategy that writes every product to the cache whenever it changes, with no expiration.",
   "Deploy Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) in front of the RDS database.",
   "Use a lazy-loading (cache-aside) strategy with a TTL of 5 minutes on each cached item.",
   "Deploy a single-node Amazon ElastiCache cluster sized for the highest expected peak.",
   "Use Amazon ElastiCache Serverless for Valkey as the cache."],
c="CE",
w=[("Lazy loading with TTL (C)", "Cache-aside loads an item only on a cache miss, so only requested data is cached; a 5-minute TTL bounds staleness to the accepted limit."),
   ("ElastiCache Serverless (E)", "ElastiCache Serverless scales automatically with demand and is replicated across multiple AZs, so no capacity planning is needed."),
   ("Read-heavy fit", "With a 50:1 read ratio and hot items, the cache absorbs most reads and protects RDS.")],
x={"A": "Write-through without expiration caches every product, including those never requested, wasting memory.",
   "B": "DAX works only with DynamoDB, not RDS.",
   "D": "A single node sized for peak requires capacity planning, wastes capacity off-peak, and is a single point of failure."},
e=[("Caching strategies", "Lazy loading (only requested data, may be stale -> add TTL) vs write-through (always fresh, caches everything)."),
   ("No cache capacity planning", "ElastiCache Serverless (Valkey, Redis OSS, Memcached).")]))
